---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Page path="/privacy/" ads={false}>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Short version: your images never leave your device. Here is the long version, including what the website itself can see.
  </p>

  <h2>Your images</h2>
  <p>
    KVGrainy is a set of static files. When you add an image, your browser reads it and a background worker in this tab does all
    the work. The page has no server that accepts uploads, so there is nowhere for an image to go. The page's security policy
    (the Content-Security-Policy) allows connections to its own origin only, and an automated test, run on every change,
    checks that no request carries image data and none goes to another host.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your theme choice</strong> (Dark, Light, Neon or a Visual Assault theme), in your browser's local storage, so the page
      looks the same next time. Nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      Results you create stay in this tab's memory only. Closing or refreshing the page clears them, so download what you want to
      keep.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cookies, accounts and analytics</h2>
  <p>
    None. No cookies are set, there are no accounts, and there is no analytics or tracking script today. No third-party scripts,
    fonts or images are loaded.
  </p>

  <h2>What the host can see</h2>
  <p>
    The site is hosted on Cloudflare. Like any website, the host that serves these files can see that a browser requested a page,
    with its IP address and browser details, in ordinary server logs, and Cloudflare's own privacy policy covers what it does with
    that. It never sees your images, because they are never sent.
  </p>

  <h2>If that changes</h2>
  <p>
    If ads or analytics are ever added, this page will be updated first to say what they collect and which cookies they set, and
    visitors in places that require it will be asked for consent before anything is loaded. Ads, if any, would never appear on the
    tool page or near its buttons.
  </p>

  <h2>The desktop app</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop app works on files on your computer and never sends them anywhere. Its only network request is a check for a newer
    version, made when it starts and when you press "Check for updates": it asks GitHub for the latest release, and sends nothing
    about you or your files beyond what any web request includes (such as your IP address).
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>.</p>
</Page>
